## classification.rules — Apache Jena generic-rule-syntax counterpart of
## gold/rules_reference.py. This is the authoritative, portable artifact
## (medallion_rdf_ido_strategy_mapping.md §6, §10 step 4): the artifact a
## real Jena/Fuseki deployment loads, one-to-one against the Python reference
## implementation this PoC actually runs and unit-tests. Diff the two on any
## change; both must be validated against the same data before either is
## trusted — gold/jena_parity.py is that check, run live against the
## `gold-rules` Fuseki service (fuseki/gold-rules.ttl).
##
## Scope (medallion §6 hybrid): LOCAL, node-local classification and
## directional guards only. The global connected-fragment partition
## (walk.py's fragment discovery, the C1/C2 consumer trace, fragment-merge)
## is NOT expressed here — SPARQL/Jena forward-chaining is awkward at
## "transitive closure that must stop at a node with a property"
## (medallion §6 point 5), and that partition stays in Silver/Python where
## it is already validated to ~97% source agreement.
##
## Reads ONLY graph:refdata and graph:masterdata. NEVER graph:oracle — the
## gold-rules service's base model is built from exactly two exported files
## (fuseki/rules-input/{masterdata,refdata}.nt), so the oracle is not merely
## unread, it is absent from the reasoner's input. gold/oracle_guard.py
## still enforces the same invariant on the Python side.
##
## Revision 2026-09-23 (first live-deployment prep). Four changes against
## the inspection-only first version, each bringing a rule into line with
## the Python reference it claims to mirror:
##   1. steamCondensateCategory: Jena documents regex() as matching a
##      literal's whole lexical form, so 'Steam|Condensate' risks matching
##      only the bare words. Now '.*(Steam|Condensate).*' — the substring
##      test data_specification.md §3.4 specifies, correct whether the
##      builtin does a whole-string or a find-anywhere match.
##   2. reliefAttribution: regex(?role, 'relief') applied a literal builtin
##      to a URI node (the role) — at best fragile, at worst never true.
##      Now binds the relief role by its exact URI (rdf_mapper.
##      map_boundary_sets mints pidsys:boundary_role/<role>; jena_parity's
##      preflight checks that URI exists in refdata).
##   3. flareGuardSkip: dropped the fragment component's own fluid and the
##      notEqual(?fluid, ?nbFluid) test. rules_reference.flare_guard reads
##      only the NEIGHBOUR's fluid; walk.py's caller only ever asks about
##      different-fluid neighbours, so downstream behaviour is unchanged,
##      but the extra conditions broke the one-to-one claim.
##   4. Each guard now also asserts its own reason predicate
##      (skipFlareSink / skipSupplyTieIn) next to the shared
##      skipAsConsumerSignal, so a parity run can compare guard-by-guard,
##      the way the notebook's §6b reports them.
##   5. (first live run) Adjacency is ido:connectedTo, not pidsys:isConnectedTo.
##      The projection moved to IDO's native, symmetric connectedTo
##      (specs/semantics/pidsys_extension.ttl: "no pidsys: connectivity
##      property is minted"); pidsys:isConnectedTo had 0 triples, so every
##      guard and the relief rule matched nothing. jena_parity's preflight now
##      fails if any predicate the Python rules read is absent from these
##      rule bodies, or any rule-body predicate has no data.

@prefix pidsys: <https://pidsys.example/ns#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix ido:    <https://www.omg.org/spec/Commons/IndustrialData/> .

# --- Fluid classification [data_specification.md §3.4] ---------------------
# Python's classify_fluid_category returns 'utility' for anything not
# matched below; here that is the ABSENCE of a selfOwningClass triple (a
# default-by-negation rule would race the positive rules under RETE).

# A fluid with Category "Flare" is self-owning: forms its own flare
# collection system, never traced to a consumer.
[flareCategory:
  (?fluid pidsys:category "Flare")
  ->
  (?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass "flare")
]

# Category "Utility" with a Subcategory containing "Steam" or "Condensate"
# (substring — also accepts a combined "Steam / Condensate" spelling).
[steamCondensateCategory:
  (?fluid pidsys:category "Utility")
  (?fluid pidsys:subcategory ?sub)
  regex(?sub, '.*(Steam|Condensate).*')
  ->
  (?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass "steam_condensate")
]

[processCategory:
  (?fluid pidsys:category "Process")
  noValue(?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass ?any)
  ->
  (?fluid pidsys:selfOwningClass "process")
]

# --- Flare guard [algorithm_spec.md §7.2] -----------------------------------
# If a fragment component's flow runs INTO a flare-fluid neighbour, and NOT
# the reverse, the neighbour is a discharge sink, not a consumer signal.
[flareGuardSkip:
  (?comp ido:connectedTo ?nb)
  (?nb pidsys:partOf ?nbSeg) (?nbSeg pidsys:fluidCode ?nbFluid)
  (?nbFluidNode rdf:type pidsys:Fluid)
  (?nbFluidNode pidsys:fluidCode ?nbFluid)
  (?nbFluidNode pidsys:selfOwningClass "flare")
  (?comp pidsys:flowsTo ?nb)
  noValue(?nb pidsys:flowsTo ?comp)
  ->
  (?comp pidsys:skipAsConsumerSignal ?nb)
  (?comp pidsys:skipFlareSink ?nb)
]

# --- Directional consumer guard [algorithm_spec.md §7.2a] -------------------
# Generalises the flare guard: if the NEIGHBOUR's flow runs into the
# fragment component (and not the reverse), the neighbour is a supply tying
# in — not a consumer this fragment feeds.
[directionalConsumerGuardSkip:
  (?comp ido:connectedTo ?nb)
  (?nb pidsys:flowsTo ?comp)
  noValue(?comp pidsys:flowsTo ?nb)
  ->
  (?comp pidsys:skipAsConsumerSignal ?nb)
  (?comp pidsys:skipSupplyTieIn ?nb)
]

# --- Relief-device attribution [algorithm_spec.md §7.4a] --------------------
# A relief device commissions with the side it protects (flow INTO the
# valve), not the discharge side (flow OUT of the valve). Asserts every
# qualifying neighbour; rules_reference.relief_attribution returns the first
# one it finds — jena_parity reports a valve with 2+ as its own category.
[reliefAttribution:
  (<https://pidsys.example/ns#boundary_role/relief> pidsys:boundaryMember ?cls)
  (?valve rdf:type ?cls)
  (?valve ido:connectedTo ?protectedSide)
  (?protectedSide pidsys:flowsTo ?valve)
  noValue(?valve pidsys:flowsTo ?protectedSide)
  ->
  (?valve pidsys:protectedBy ?protectedSide)
]
